Let the GPU process open the V4L2 decoder it is about to enumerate.

use_v4l2_codec=true compiles the V4L2 decoders in, and on mainline Linux
media/gpu/v4l2 finds the decoder by walking /dev/video0..255 with
QUERYCAP, plus /dev/media0..9 to match the media controller the stateless
decoder needs for the request API. Both walks happen inside the GPU
process, after the sandbox is up, so every open() goes through the
syscall broker.

The broker's permission list does not contain any of those paths.
FilePermissionsForGpu() calls AddV4L2GpuPermissions() only under
IsChromeOS() or the Chromecast allowlist -- a plain Linux build reaches
AddStandardGpuPermissions() and nothing else -- and even that function
authorizes only ChromeOS's /dev/video-dec[0-4] and /dev/media-dec[0-4]
udev names, which no mainline kernel creates.

So a correctly configured V4L2 build finds no decoder, reports no
hardware decode, and falls back to software with nothing in the log to
say a device was ever refused. Authorize the ranges the enumeration
actually probes, on non-ChromeOS builds that compiled V4L2 in and were
not asked to disable accelerated video.

WHAT THIS WIDENS, SAID PLAINLY. ChromeOS restricts the broker to the
video-dec* prefix precisely so that "we cannot authorize a non-decoder
device by accident" -- its own comment. A number-blind range cannot make
that promise: on this phone /dev/video0..5 are camss capture nodes, and
after this patch the GPU process may open them. That is a real widening,
and it is the narrower of the two options actually available, the other
being --no-sandbox, which is what people reach for today and which gives
up the entire policy rather than one device class.

The narrow fix exists and is bigger than this. FilePermissionsForGpu()
runs BEFORE the sandbox is engaged -- GpuPreSandboxHook() calls it on the
way to StartBrokerProcess() -- so it may QUERYCAP the nodes itself and
authorize only those that answer as a decoder, which is the same test
V4L2Device::EnumerateDevicesForType() already implements a few
translation units away and which this file can already reach (it
includes media/gpu/v4l2/v4l2_device.h under the same buildflag). Doing
it there would also warm that enumeration's static cache while opening
is still unrestricted. Left for a follow-up rather than smuggled into a
patch whose point is to make the feature work at all.

--- a/content/common/gpu_pre_sandbox_hook_linux.cc
+++ b/content/common/gpu_pre_sandbox_hook_linux.cc
@@ -191,6 +191,49 @@
   }
 }
 
+#if BUILDFLAG(USE_V4L2_CODEC) && !BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
+// Mainline Linux has none of the /dev/video-dec*, /dev/media-dec* or
+// /dev/jpeg-* symlinks that AddV4L2GpuPermissions() above authorizes: those
+// are a ChromeOS udev convention. On an ordinary kernel the decoder is just
+// some /dev/videoN, behind whatever else the SoC enumerates first -- on
+// msm8998 the venus decoder is /dev/video7, behind six camss capture nodes --
+// and it is identified by its capabilities, never by its number.
+//
+// That is exactly what media/gpu/v4l2 does on this platform:
+//
+//   V4L2Device::EnumerateDevicesForType() walks /dev/video0 .. /dev/video255
+//   and QUERYCAPs each one (kMaxDevices = 256, non-ChromeOS branch), and
+//   V4L2Device::GetRequestsQueue() walks /dev/media0 .. /dev/media9 to find
+//   the media controller matching the decoder by bus_info -- which is how the
+//   stateless decoder reaches the V4L2 request API at all.
+//
+// Neither walk can open anything the broker was not told about, so a build
+// with use_v4l2_codec=true silently finds no decoder and falls back to
+// software decoding. Authorize the ranges the code actually probes.
+void AddV4L2MainlineGpuPermissions(
+    std::vector<BrokerFilePermission>* permissions,
+    const sandbox::policy::SandboxSeccompBPF::Options& options) {
+  if (!options.accelerated_video_decode_enabled &&
+      !options.accelerated_video_encode_enabled) {
+    return;
+  }
+
+  // Keep in sync with kMaxDevices in V4L2Device::EnumerateDevicesForType().
+  static constexpr int kMaxV4L2VideoNodes = 256;
+  for (int i = 0; i < kMaxV4L2VideoNodes; ++i) {
+    permissions->push_back(
+        BrokerFilePermission::ReadWrite(base::StringPrintf("/dev/video%d", i)));
+  }
+
+  // Keep in sync with the loop in V4L2Device::GetRequestsQueue().
+  static constexpr int kMaxV4L2MediaNodes = 10;
+  for (int i = 0; i < kMaxV4L2MediaNodes; ++i) {
+    permissions->push_back(
+        BrokerFilePermission::ReadWrite(base::StringPrintf("/dev/media%d", i)));
+  }
+}
+#endif  // BUILDFLAG(USE_V4L2_CODEC) && !BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
+
 void AddArmMaliGpuPermissions(std::vector<BrokerFilePermission>* permissions) {
   // Device file needed by the ARM GPU userspace.
   static const char kMali0Path[] = "/dev/mali0";
@@ -668,6 +711,12 @@
     }
   }
 
+#if BUILDFLAG(USE_V4L2_CODEC) && !BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
+  if (UseV4L2Codec(options)) {
+    AddV4L2MainlineGpuPermissions(&permissions, options);
+  }
+#endif
+
   AddStandardGpuPermissions(&permissions);
   return permissions;
 }
